---
import Docs from '../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { source } from '../data/measured';

const counts = { date: '26 November 2026', unit: '1,493', library: '980', smoke: '376' };
const intro =
  'How swiftplot was built, why it will be kept up, what stage it is at, what it does not do yet and what 1.0 needs.';
const sections = [
  { id: 'how-it-was-built', label: 'How it was built' },
  { id: 'why-it-will-be-kept-up', label: 'Why it will be kept up' },
  { id: 'stage', label: 'Stage' },
  { id: 'what-it-does-not-do-yet', label: 'What it does not do yet' },
  { id: 'what-1-0-needs', label: 'What 1.0 needs' },
];
const sidebar = [
  {
    label: 'Also',
    links: [
      { href: '/guides/getting-started', label: 'Getting started' },
      { href: '/examples', label: 'Examples' },
      { href: '/reference', label: 'Reference' },
      { href: '/changelog', label: 'Changelog' },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Docs title="Status · swiftplot" description={intro} sidebar={sidebar} sections={sections}>
  <h1>Status</h1>
  <p class="intro">{intro}</p>

  <h2 id="how-it-was-built">How it was built</h2>
  <p>
    swiftplot was written with AI coding tools, directed and reviewed by its author. What stands behind it is what
    can be checked:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      {counts.unit} tests, {counts.library} of them for the library and its React and Vue wrappers, the rest for this
      site and the release scripts.
    </li>
    <li>
      {counts.smoke} browser tests that load the built site in Chromium and check that its charts draw, that nothing
      logs an error and that its internal links resolve.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="https://github.com/gusevp/chart-bench">chart-bench</a>, a benchmark that draws the same charts with
      several libraries in the same browser. The README has <a href={source}>the tables and the method</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      A survey of the issue trackers of other chart libraries, for the problems their users report again and again,
      which the design was checked against.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>The counts are from the test runners on {counts.date}. Both suites run on every pull request.</p>

  <h2 id="why-it-will-be-kept-up">Why it will be kept up</h2>
  <p>
    swiftplot began because the chart library in a product I work on stopped releasing. That product now draws its
    charts with swiftplot, so I depend on it the same way you would: a bug you hit is one my users hit too, and I fix
    it for my own sake. I read every issue. If I ever stop working on it, I will say so here and in the README rather
    than go quiet.
  </p>

  <h2 id="stage">Stage</h2>
  <p>
    swiftplot is at 0.x. The API may change from one release to the next, and every change is in the{' '}
    <a href="/changelog">changelog</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-it-does-not-do-yet">What it does not do yet</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <b>No export to PNG.</b> Axis text is DOM, not canvas text, so an export has to draw the text onto the canvas
      first.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>The data is not exposed to assistive technology.</b> A chart is an image with a text label, set by its
      <code>description</code> option; a screen reader cannot read the values.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-1-0-needs">What 1.0 needs</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Three minor releases in a row with no breaking change to the API.</li>
    <li>Export to PNG, with the axis text drawn in.</li>
    <li>Charts a screen reader can read: the values, not only a label.</li>
    <li>Used in production by someone other than me.</li>
  </ul>
</Docs>
